meta {
  name: Marking who was there
  seq: 13
}

docs {
  Attendance is kept twice over and the two are not derived from each other. A
  period mark says a pupil was in that class in that period; a day mark says
  the pupil was in school that forenoon or afternoon. Schools need both - one
  for the subject teacher's record and the truancy that hides inside a full
  day, one for the roll the office reports upwards - and neither is computed
  from the other, so a pupil can be present all day and absent from third
  period without any contradiction to resolve.

  The natural key of a period mark is (student_id, attendance_date,
  period_id). SUBJECT OFFERING IS NOT IN IT. One pupil is in one place in one
  period, so submitting the same register twice is an idempotent upsert on
  that key rather than a duplicate, and the create/update counts in the
  response say which happened. It also means marking the same pupil for the
  same period under two different offerings overwrites rather than accumulates
  - which is correct, and is the reason the clash rules exist.

  Four fields are the server's, not yours: timetable_slot_id and teacher_id
  are resolved from the occurrence that stands on the day, and marked_by and
  marked_at come from the caller and the clock. Sending any of them is 400,
  because unknown fields are refused outright. That resolution is also why a
  register cannot be taken against a class that is not timetabled - there
  would be no slot to record.

  Every refusal that is about the school day rather than the request is 422
  with a code in the message: attendance_not_in_session,
  attendance_not_scheduled, attendance_class_cancelled,
  attendance_not_enrolled. A mark the caller cannot reach is 404, never 403,
  so a grant's reach cannot be used to prove a mark exists.

  The second half of this folder tears down folder 12. Every timetable row
  above is a precondition of something here, so it could not be deleted there;
  and it has to go before folder 14 deletes the section, the offering and the
  pupil underneath it.
}
